"""
Vector sample block: continuous vector input -> clocked vector output.

Dimension-preserving clock-crossing block, templated like
`DiscreteComponents.Examples.MatrixGain` (structural dimension parameter,
array I/O via comprehension). Wraps `n` independent scalar
`DiscreteComponents.Sampler` blocks, one per channel, so an `n`-wide
continuous signal is sampled into an `n`-wide clocked signal.

Clock-agnostic: the output clock of each channel is inferred from what the
corresponding `y[i]` is connected to (place a `PeriodicClock` on the clocked
side of the crossing, or feed a clocked block such as `DiscreteStateSpace`
downstream). Unlike `MatrixGain`, a sampler is not a linear map, so there is a
single channel-count `n` rather than separate `ny`/`nu`.

Connectors:
- `u :: RealInput[n]`  (continuous) — input signal.
- `y :: RealOutput[n]` (clocked)    — sampled output signal.
"""
component MultiSampler
  "Continuous input connectors"
  u = [RealInput() for i in 1:n] {^u}
  "Clocked output connectors"
  y = [RealOutput() for i in 1:n] {^y}
  "Per-channel scalar samplers"
  s = [DiscreteComponents.Sampler() for i in 1:n]
  "Number of channels (dimension preserved across the block)"
  structural parameter n::Integer = 1
relations
  for i in 1:n
    connect(u[i], s[i].u)
    connect(s[i].y, y[i])
  end
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://F16ModelWorkshop/multi_sampler.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 0, "y1": 450, "x2": 100, "y2": 550, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "y": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 900, "y1": 450, "x2": 1000, "y2": 550, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Vector zero-order-hold block: clocked vector input -> continuous vector output.

Dimension-preserving clock-crossing block, templated like
`DiscreteComponents.Examples.MatrixGain` (structural dimension parameter,
array I/O via comprehension). Wraps `n` independent scalar
`DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold` blocks, one per channel, so an `n`-wide
clocked signal is held into an `n`-wide continuous signal.

Clock-agnostic: the input clock of each channel is inferred from what the
corresponding `u[i]` is connected to (typically a clocked block output such as
`DiscreteStateSpace.y`). Like `MultiSampler`, a hold is dimension-preserving, so
there is a single channel-count `n`.

Connectors:
- `u :: RealInput[n]`  (clocked)    — input signal.
- `y :: RealOutput[n]` (continuous) — held output signal.
"""
component MultiZeroOrderHold
  "Clocked input connectors"
  u = [RealInput() for i in 1:n] {^u}
  "Continuous output connectors"
  y = [RealOutput() for i in 1:n] {^y}
  "Per-channel scalar zero-order holds"
  z = [DiscreteComponents.ZeroOrderHold() for i in 1:n]
  "Number of channels (dimension preserved across the block)"
  structural parameter n::Integer = 1
relations
  for i in 1:n
    connect(u[i], z[i].u)
    connect(z[i].y, y[i])
  end
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://F16ModelWorkshop/multi_zero_order_hold.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 0, "y1": 450, "x2": 100, "y2": 550, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    },
    "y": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {
          "diagram": {"iconName": "default", "x1": 900, "y1": 450, "x2": 1000, "y2": 550, "rot": 0}
        },
        "tags": []
      }
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Minimal validation of the vector clock-crossing pair.

A single continuous ramp feeds a 1-channel `MultiSampler`; the sampled signal is
held back to continuous by a 1-channel `MultiZeroOrderHold`. A 10 Hz
`PeriodicClock` is planted on the clocked junction. A downstream `Integrator`
consumes the held signal so the otherwise purely-clocked model carries a real
ODE state (an empty `u0` crashes the auto-dt initializer).

Expected: `retcode == Success`; `intg.x` accumulates the zero-order-hold
staircase of the ramp sampled every 0.1 s (per-interval increments
`[0.0, 0.1, ..., 0.9] * 0.1`, total 0.45 at t = 1 s).

A single channel is sufficient to validate the wrapper mechanics (array
comprehension, clock inference, sample-hold physics). The multi-channel vector
path is exercised end-to-end by `F16DiscreteClosedLoop`, where the controller's
shared block clock unifies all channels.
"""
test component TestSampledIO
  "Ramp: 0 -> 1 over 1 s."
  src1 = BlockComponents.Sources.Ramp(height = 1.0, duration = 1.0)
  "Continuous -> clocked (1 channel)."
  sample = MultiSampler(n = 1)
  "Clocked -> continuous (1 channel)."
  zoh = MultiZeroOrderHold(n = 1)
  "Sample clock (10 Hz)."
  clk = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = 0.1)
  """
  Continuous consumer of the held signal: provides a real ODE state so the
   purely-clocked model is not state-empty (empty u0 crashes the auto-dt
   initializer). Its state is the running integral of the ZOH staircase.
  """
  intg = BlockComponents.Continuous.Integrator()
relations
  connect(src1.y, sample.u[1])
  # Plant the clock on the single clocked junction.
  connect(sample.y[1], zoh.u[1], clk.y)
  connect(zoh.y[1], intg.u)
end

analysis TestSampledIOTransient
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 1.0)
  model = TestSampledIO()
relations
end